# RISC0 Receipt and Proof Handling

## Context
- Applied when working with RISC0 v1.2.1 receipt types
- Focuses on correct method usage for different receipt variants
- Ensures consistent access to seal bytes and other receipt data
- Handles MaybePruned types correctly for claims and digests
- See 2001-risc0-api for high-level RISC0 integration guidelines
- See 2002-risc0-types for import standards

## Requirements
- Use correct methods for accessing receipt data:
  - Use `get_seal_bytes()` ONLY on `SuccinctReceipt` types
  - For `CompositeReceipt`, first convert to `SuccinctReceipt` using `prover.composite_to_succinct()`
  - Use `receipt.claim()?.digest()` with `Digestible` trait imported for receipt digests
  - Use `receipt.inner` to match on receipt variants
  - Access `verifier_parameters` instead of `exit_code` in metadata
- Handle receipt variants appropriately:
  - Use pattern matching to handle both `Composite` and `Succinct` variants
  - ALWAYS include catch-all pattern for non-exhaustive `InnerReceipt` enum
  - Convert `CompositeReceipt` to `SuccinctReceipt` using `composite_to_succinct` before accessing seal bytes
  - Access journal data through `receipt.journal.bytes`
  - Handle unknown variants with appropriate error logging
- Handle mutability correctly:
  - Mark receipt variables as `mut` when using `std::mem::take()`
  - Use `mut` when modifying receipt fields or taking ownership
  - Ensure proper ownership transfer for journal data
  - Handle mutable references correctly in async contexts
- Handle journal data correctly:
  - Validate journal structure before processing
  - Check for expected section sizes (input digest: 32 bytes)
  - Prevent duplicate data in journal outputs
  - Use clear markers between journal sections
  - Follow the standard journal format:
    1. Input digest (32 bytes)
    2. Structured output markers
    3. Actual output data
  - Verify total committed data size matches expected
- Handle MaybePruned types correctly:
  - Import `risc0_zkvm::sha::Digestible` trait when working with `MaybePruned<ReceiptClaim>`
  - Use `claim.digest()` directly on `MaybePruned` types (don't use `as_value()`)
  - Handle potential errors from `receipt.claim()?` appropriately
- Follow RISC0 v1.2.1 receipt processing patterns:
  - Use `InnerReceipt` enum for type checking
  - Handle all receipt variants in match statements
  - Log appropriate errors for unexpected variants
  - Convert verifier parameters to exit codes correctly

## Examples

<example>
// Good: Complete receipt handling with journal validation
use risc0_zkvm::{Receipt, InnerReceipt, sha::Digestible};

fn process_receipt(mut receipt: Receipt, prover: &Prover) -> Result<Vec<u8>> {
    // Validate journal structure
    let journal_bytes = &receipt.journal.bytes;
    if journal_bytes.len() < 32 {
        return Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Invalid journal size"));
    }
    
    // Split journal into sections
    let (input_digest, remaining) = journal_bytes.split_at(32);
    
    // Process remaining data ensuring no duplicates
    let mut seen_markers = HashSet::new();
    let mut current_pos = 0;
    while current_pos < remaining.len() {
        let marker = remaining[current_pos];
        if !seen_markers.insert(marker) {
            return Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Duplicate section marker"));
        }
        // Process section...
    }
    
    // Handle receipt type
    match &receipt.inner {
        InnerReceipt::Composite(cr) => {
            let sr = prover.composite_to_succinct(&cr)?;
            let journal = std::mem::take(&mut receipt.journal);
            Ok(sr.get_seal_bytes().to_vec())
        }
        InnerReceipt::Succinct(sr) => {
            let journal = std::mem::take(&mut receipt.journal);
            Ok(sr.get_seal_bytes().to_vec())
        }
        _ => {
            error!("Unknown receipt type encountered");
            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProvingFailed,
                error => "unknown_receipt_type"
            );
            Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Unknown receipt type"))
        }
    }
}
</example>

<example>
// Good: Structured journal output handling
fn write_to_journal(env: &mut ExecutorEnv, data: &[u8], marker: u8) -> Result<()> {
    // Write section marker
    env.write(&[marker])?;
    
    // Write actual data
    env.write(data)?;
    
    Ok(())
}

fn process_outputs(env: &mut ExecutorEnv, outputs: &[Vec<u8>]) -> Result<()> {
    // Write input digest first
    let input_hash = sha::digest(&inputs);
    env.write(&input_hash)?;
    
    // Write each output section with unique markers
    for (i, output) in outputs.iter().enumerate() {
        write_to_journal(env, output, 0xA0 + i as u8)?;
    }
    
    Ok(())
}
</example>

